{
  "about": "YAML through Clarity.Formats in code (docs/FORMATS-PLAN.md, \"Step 5: YAML\"): `read list<Order> from yaml text at \"orders\"` and `yaml of orders at \"orders\"`. YAML is syntax only: YAML 1.2's core schema read into the one document model as JSON's values are (a mapping is an object, a sequence a list), so paths, `at`, mappings, fitting and the messages are the engine's, as for JSON, with \"YAML\" in them. Reading: block mappings and sequences, flow `{}` and `[]`, plain, single- and double-quoted scalars (escapes, folding), block scalars `|` and `>` with chomping `-` and `+`, comments, anchors and aliases (a copy of what the anchor names), the first document of a stream (`%` directives and `---` before it, `...` or the next `---` after it); tags are left aside; complex keys (`?`) are refused. A plain scalar is a number (an integer or a float, kept as the JSON number it is), `true` or `false` (also `True`, `TRUE`), null (`null`, `~` or nothing), else text; `.inf` and `.nan` stay text; a key is its text as written. What is no YAML is `the text is no YAML at line N`. Writing: block style, two spaces, keys in order, a list item's first key on its dash's line; a text in double quotes (with escapes) where it would read back as something else (number-like, true, false or null, a leading indicator, `: ` or ` #` inside, a trailing `:`, a leading or trailing space, empty, a control character), a text of several lines as a `|` block (`|-` without a final line break, `|+` with more than one); an empty list `[]`, an empty record `{}`; the file ends with a line break. `read` cases read and fit `kind`; `write` cases write `value` as `kind` and give `written` byte for byte; `roundTrip` cases write `value` and read it back with the same `at` to the same value. A file source written back keeping its comments is in formats-table.json.",
